>>
>>>>> should also clarify that I mean going from a JSON serialized C++ class
>>>>> (or similar) to the C++ class itself --- that's what's (moderately)
>>>>> painful.
>>>>
>>>> I think that's pretty much impossible in general, since C++ classes
>>>> can be anything.   We could write specific cases if the data's all
>>>> strings or numbers.
>>>
>>> But in the specific case of var_context, Protocol Buffers could do it
>>> easily.
>>
>> Agreed.  I didn't realize that's what we were talking about.
>> How was Eigen related to this?
>>
>
> No -- more or less separate. It seems that the chains are too large to
> serialize, no matter how efficiently one goes about it.
>
>>
>>> Remember what var_context is:
>>>
>>>     std::map<std::string,
>>>              std::pair<std::vector<double>,
>>>                        std::vector<size_t> > > vars_r_;
>>>     std::map<std::string,
>>>              std::pair<std::vector<int>,
>>>                        std::vector<size_t> > > vars_i_;
>>>     std::vector<double> const empty_vec_r_;
>>>     std::vector<int> const empty_vec_i_;
>>>     std::vector<size_t> const empty_vec_ui_;
>>
>> I like to think of the var_context as an interface.  That's
>> a specific implementation of var_context I used for the dump
>> data format.
>>
>> I think if we went to something like JSON or protocol buffers,
>> then following the shape of the data would be more natural
>> than this mess, which I only introduced for convenience for the
>> case at hand.
>>
>>
>>> Serializing and deserializing (binary or text) is the sort of thing
>>> Protocol Buffers was made for as far as I can tell.
>>
>> Yes, but only for structured data like this --- not for general
>> C++ classes, which can have anything in them, like pointers and
>> references and file handles and ...  That's why I was confused
>> earlier.
>
> You're right -- function pointers and file handles need to be handled
> separately. But I don't think we pass file handles except for the stderr
> stream, right?
>
> Just to check, I'm looking at the stan_args configuration class in R --
> I think the function and file pointers are already handled separately. I
> think the entire thing could be Protocol Buffer/JSON-ized. (In some
> respects this seems easier than var_context.)
>
> Data in the C++ class rstan::stan_args (which is, btw, 600 LOC):
>
>    unsigned int random_seed;
>    unsigned int chain_id;
>    std::string init;
>    SEXP init_list;
>    double init_radius;
>    std::string sample_file; // the file for outputting the samples
>    bool append_samples;
>    bool sample_file_flag; // true: write out to a file; false, do not
>    stan_args_method_t method;
>    std::string diagnostic_file;
>    bool diagnostic_file_flag;
>    union {
>      struct {
>        int iter;   // number of iterations
>        int refresh;  //
>        sampling_algo_t algorithm;
>        int warmup; // number of warmup
>        int thin;
>        bool save_warmup; // weather to save warmup samples (always true now)
>        int iter_save; // number of iterations saved
>        int iter_save_wo_warmup; // number of iterations saved wo warmup
>        bool adapt_engaged;
>        double adapt_gamma;
>        double adapt_delta;
>        double adapt_kappa;
>        unsigned int adapt_init_buffer;
>        unsigned int adapt_term_buffer;
>        unsigned int adapt_window;
>        double adapt_t0;
>        sampling_metric_t metric; // UNIT_E, DIAG_E, DENSE_E;
>        double stepsize; // defaut to 1;
>        double stepsize_jitter;
>        int max_treedepth; // for NUTS, default to 10.
>        double int_time; // for HMC, default to 2 * pi
>      } sampling;
>      struct {
>        int iter; // default to 2000
>        int refresh; // default to 100
>        optim_algo_t algorithm; // Newton, (L)BFGS
>        bool save_iterations; // default to false
>        double init_alpha; // default to 0.001, for (L)BFGS
>        double tol_obj; // default to 1e-12, for (L)BFGS
>        double tol_grad; // default to 1e-8, for (L)BFGS
>        double tol_param; // default to 1e-8, for (L)BFGS
>        double tol_rel_obj; // default to 1e4, for (L)BFGS
>        double tol_rel_grad; // default to 1e7, for (L)BFGS
>        int history_size; // default to 5, for LBFGS only
>      } optim;
>      struct {
>        double epsilon; // default to 1e-6, for test_grad
>        double error;  // default to 1e-6, for test_grad
>      } test_grad;
>    } ctrl;
