From ua5e83218c834e013 at groups.google.com  Sat Mar  7 10:53:26 2015
From: ua5e83218c834e013 at groups.google.com (Michael Betancourt)
Date: Sat, 07 Mar 2015 10:53:26 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-dev] "Matt trick" vs. "non-centered parameterization"
Message-ID: <9ZvhKpXlwuI.6ZC4C1wXDF0J@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <9ZvhKpXlwuI.-90jl-ox3LYJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
References: <9ZvhKpXlwuI.fFBeMh-2N-8J@stan-dev.groups.google.com> <9ZvhKpXlwuI.-90jl-ox3LYJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/9ZvhKpXlwuI/m/6ZC4C1wXDF0J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

This is basically the correct perspective.  Remember, that the posterior
is a probability _measure_, which is a parameterization-invariant, abstract
mathematical object.  The Stan Modeling Language defines a
probability _density_, which is a non-unique, parameterization-dependent
function from R^{n} -> R.  What this means is that a given model can be
represented different ways in Stan, and different representations have
different computational performances.

There’s nothing implicit or explicit or whatnot going on.  It’s just the fundamental
ambiguity of trying to define measures via densities.

