Ben --- can you validate that adding the following to

  src/stan/math/rev/core/var.hpp

after the Windows ifdef will allow you to compile:

#if (__GNUC__ > 4) || ((__GNUC__ == 4) && (__GNUC__MINOR >= 3))
      // in GNUC 4.6 and beyond, we need to allow __float128
      // due to some combination of Eigen and Boost
      var(__float128 x) : vi_(new vari(static_cast<double>(x))) { }
#endif

The other tack we could take would be fixing lkj_corr, which seems
to be the source of the issue.

It just gets ignored on my Mac so there's no point putting in a
pull request until we can make sure it works on the platforms its
intended to fix. It now seems we need to get linux into our auto-tested
stack, and as far as that goes, we really need to test multiple
versions of GCC.  What a mess!

I'm a bit worried by

  https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Floating-Types.html

which states

  Not all targets support additional floating-point types.
  __float80 and __float128 types are supported on x86 and IA-64
  targets. The __float128 type is supported on hppa HP-UX targets.

I have no idea if anyone (including CRAN) will be testing hardware
other than Intel.

- Bob

