Rob has been talking about implementing a new testing framework which will make:
1) developing new functions easier to test
2) make our existing functions more robust by making it easier to test boundary conditions
3) cleaning up a lot of existing test code.

Seth, if you're still thinking about implementing kronecker utilities, you should help out on this effort. It'll make your life easier in the long run.

From u2a7f346506a3e9ff at groups.google.com  Thu Mar 12 16:04:48 2015
From: u2a7f346506a3e9ff at groups.google.com (Marcus Brubaker)
Date: Thu, 12 Mar 2015 16:04:48 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-dev] Re: rebase?
Message-ID: <UDfC5EUCiw8.r0UavkO434cJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <UDfC5EUCiw8.d0IMy1s4TXEJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
References: <UDfC5EUCiw8.-7zYJqcUdbwJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com> <UDfC5EUCiw8.d0IMy1s4TXEJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/UDfC5EUCiw8/m/r0UavkO434cJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Rebasing is fine if it's done on branches that haven't been pushed at all.  Once they've been pushed, they're public in a sense and other people can end up relying on their history.  Rebasing at this stage will break that history and require --force to push.  The issue really is that we often push our branches early.  Personally, I think this is good habit, as it backs up the branch.  It is also the most convenient way to share branches across multiple machines.  E.G., I use around three different machines for Stan development sometimes (office, laptop, server, etc).

One solution is to have a separate personal repository where no one can depend on your branch and you can break history at will.  Then, only when you're ready to merge and run testing, do you push the branch to the main Stan repo.  That said, I personally don't think it's worth the trouble for what is inherently a cosmetic issue.

Cheers,
Marcus

