> > On Mar 28, 2015, at 3:18 AM, Allen B. Riddell wrote:
> >
> > ...
>
> > The cache
> > could hold 100,000+ distinct models.
>
> While we could afford the storage of 100K * 10MB (that's
> still only 1TB), it would present a difficult organization
> problem.  I'm uncomfortable officially distributing models
> that we don't vet.
>
> One place to start would be to go through the BUGS models and
> make sure they're all up to our recommended practices.
> That'll get us up to about 75 or something like that.  Then
> repeat 1500 times :-)
>
> - Bob

I would be much more likely to contribute models I'm interested in rather than trying to get some arbitrary BUGS-related collection working.  The barrier for me contributing standardized models (in addition to time) is that there's no clear standard to follow... and so far no easy framework for vetting that a pile of models works.  For me that was part of the reason for getting the X*b thing working well since so many models can be expressed that way:

http://www.amazon.com/Richly-Parameterized-Linear-Models-Statistical/dp/143986683X/

Krzysztof

