Two other contenders to throw in the mix: heap-allocated vectors (vector<T>*) or heap-allocated strings (string<T>*) to save the copy until C++11?    String ensures contiguity and can be accessed directly via string::data() when needed.  (std::vector is contiguous but not by spec, tbb::concurrent_vector for example is not contiguous, std::string is contiguous by spec & impl.)

Related, an issue that's bitten me with C++14: VLAs go away in C++14 (and maybe come back in C++17), so using C-style arrays may become problematic in the future in a C++14 move.  (double* d will be fine, but double d[] might not.)

