I believe std::vector is required to be contiguous.
There's a C++11 method to get the data directory (rather
than as &x[0]), documented here:

http://www.cplusplus.com/reference/vector/vector/data/

Agreed that we should stay away from variable-length arrays.

I'd rather stay away with heap allocation that's not encapsulated
in initialization (the RIIA pattern), because I don't want to
have to track down memory leaks.

- Bob

> On Mar 31, 2015, at 1:41 AM, Ido Rosen <iro...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> Two other contenders to throw in the mix: heap-allocated vectors (vector<T>*) or heap-allocated strings (string<T>*) to save the copy until C++11?    String ensures contiguity and can be accessed directly via string::data() when needed.  (std::vector is contiguous but not by spec, tbb::concurrent_vector for example is not contiguous, std::string is contiguous by spec & impl.)
>
> Related, an issue that's bitten me with C++14: VLAs go away in C++14 (and maybe come back in C++17), so using C-style arrays may become problematic in the future in a C++14 move.  (double* d will be fine, but double d[] might not.)
>
> On Mon, Mar 30, 2015 at 10:23 AM, Michael Betancourt <betan...@gmail.com> wrote:
> What about arrays of strings (such as parameter names)?
> Right now I’m using a vector<string> but would a string* array be easier?
>
